# Extensions production's PGDATA actually contains — #2661.
#
# This file is the EXPECTATION the monthly restore drill asserts against. It
# exists because `deploy/postgres/Dockerfile`'s package list is a claim about
# what a restore needs, and nothing checked it: #974 moved the base image off
# expired bullseye and installed the two packages the product uses explicitly,
# correctly — and with that silently stopped supplying
# `postgis_tiger_geocoder`, which the old `postgis/postgis` base had bundled.
# The repo's database image then could not restore the production backup for
# five weeks, with every check green, because the only thing that reads the
# package set against production's real catalogue is the drill, and it runs
# monthly.
#
# MEASURED on agrent_production, 2026-11-07, owner-authorised read-only query:
#
#   SELECT extname, extversion FROM pg_extension ORDER BY extname;
#
# Not inferred from the Dockerfile. The Dockerfile installs two packages; the
# cluster carries SEVEN extensions, and the gap is the whole defect — three of
# these arrive as dependencies or as part of a bundle rather than by explicit
# intent, which is exactly how one of them went missing without anyone
# choosing to remove it.
#
# One name per line, `#` comments ignored. Versions are deliberately NOT pinned
# here: a minor extension bump is routine and pinning would make the drill fail
# on an upgrade that is fine. The property is PRESENCE — a restore that cannot
# create an extension fails with an opaque pg_restore error, and that is what
# this turns into a named comparison.
#
# Adding a line is a claim that production needs it. Removing one is a claim
# that it does not.
#
# `postgis_tiger_geocoder` WAS here and is gone (#2662, owner-authorised
# 2026-11-07). It was dropped from production first and this line second, in
# that order, because the reverse makes the drill assert a set production does
# not have. Pre-checked before the DDL: no other extension depended on it, and
# all 8,148 rows in the tiger schemas were the extension's OWN shipped
# reference data — the PAGC address lexicon (pagc_rules 4354, pagc_lex 2938,
# pagc_gaz 835) plus loader defaults. The actual TIGER shapefile tables (addr,
# edges, faces, featnames) were empty, so nobody had ever loaded US census
# data and the drop destroyed nothing a person put there.
#
# `postgresql-16-postgis-tiger-geocoder` DELIBERATELY STAYS in the Dockerfile
# (owner ruling). Every GCE snapshot in the 14-day retention window still
# contains the extension, so a dump taken before the drop still emits
# `CREATE EXTENSION … postgis_tiger_geocoder`, and an image that cannot provide
# it cannot restore those backups — which is #2652 again, chosen this time.
# Remove the package only after the window has passed.
#
# Restoring one of those older dumps now reports the extension as EXTRA, which
# WARNS rather than fails. That asymmetry was a judgement call when the drill
# assertion landed one commit earlier; this is the case it was for.
#
# `fuzzystrmatch` is now an orphan: it was here as a tiger dependency and
# nothing in the product uses soundex or levenshtein — the fuzzy search that
# exists is pg_trgm. It stays until somebody verifies no other extension or
# function needs it. Tracked on #2662.

fuzzystrmatch
pg_trgm
plpgsql
postgis
postgis_topology
vector
